-- Issue 1150 OVER-NARROWING GUARD — the half of the fix that no pre-existing
-- test could have caught.
--
-- The fix for 1150 replaces the value restriction's first-character head test
-- with `isSome (lookupCtor env name)`, which REMOVES programs from the accepted
-- set: every alias-qualified application (`A.f x`) used to be classified as a
-- constructor application and generalized, and now is expansive like any other
-- application.  Every test written before the fix covers the old, wider
-- behaviour, so nothing in the suite can notice if the narrowing goes too far —
-- the failure mode is silent over-rejection in unrelated code that suddenly
-- stops compiling.  (`import_alias_ok` in this same corpus exercises an
-- alias-qualified call, but never in a `let`-binding position, so it does not
-- reach the value restriction at all.)
--
-- So: an alias-qualified call bound by `let` and used at a SINGLE type must
-- still typecheck AND run correctly.  It no longer generalizes — that is the
-- point of the fix — but a monomorphic binding is perfectly sound and must keep
-- working.  This is the exact program of `reject_1150_alias_qualified_head`
-- with the second, incompatible view of the table removed.
--
-- If this stops compiling, the narrowing overshot.  Verdict: ACCEPT, and `.out`
-- pins the value: `putI` writes 1 at key "k", so `getI` must read back
-- `Some 1` and print `1`.
import hash_map as H
import hash_map.{HashMap}

putI : HashMap String Int -> Unit
putI m = H.set "k" 1 m

getI : HashMap String Int -> Option Int
getI m = H.get "k" m

main =
  let m = H.new ()
  let _ = putI m
  match getI m
    Some n => println (debug n)
    None => println "none"
